{"name":"White Convolvulus","hex":"#f4f2f4","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"},{"term":"convolvulus","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several plants, of the genus Convolvulus, found in temperate climates, having small trumpet-shaped flowers.","A species of hawkmoth, Agrius convolvuli."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/convolvulus"}],"entries":[{"title":"Convolvulus arvensis","blocks":[{"text":"Convolvulus arvensis, or field bindweed, is a species of bindweed in the Convolvulaceae native to Europe and Asia. It is a rhizomatous and climbing or creeping herbaceous perennial plant with stems growing to 0.5–2 metres (1.6–6.6 ft) in length. It is usually found at ground level with small white and pink flowers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Convolvulus_arvensis_in_Aveyron_%282%29.jpg/330px-Convolvulus_arvensis_in_Aveyron_%282%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convolvulus_arvensis"},{"title":"Convolvulus","blocks":[{"text":"Convolvulus is a genus of about 200 to 250 species of flowering plants in the bindweed family Convolvulaceae, with a cosmopolitan distribution; the Plants of the World Online database currently accepts 227 species and 12 natural hybrids. Common names include bindweed and morning glory; both are names shared with other closely related genera."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Convolvulus_arvensis_bg.jpg/330px-Convolvulus_arvensis_bg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convolvulus"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Convolvulus_arvensis_Lincolnshire_2026_Flower.jpg/120px-Convolvulus_arvensis_Lincolnshire_2026_Flower.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Convolvulus_arvensis_Lincolnshire_2026_Flower.jpg/250px-Convolvulus_arvensis_Lincolnshire_2026_Flower.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Convolvulus_arvensis_Lincolnshire_2026_Flower.jpg/120px-Convolvulus_arvensis_Lincolnshire_2026_Flower.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AConvolvulus_arvensis_Lincolnshire_2026_Flower.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Convolvulus_arvensis_Lincolnshire_2026_Leaf.jpg/120px-Convolvulus_arvensis_Lincolnshire_2026_Leaf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipe